<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Mechelen</h1><p class="st-meta">1333 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Mechelen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small separate lordship between Brabant and Antwerp that became the judicial and court capital of the Burgundian and Habsburg Netherlands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mechelen was long divided between the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-berthout">Berthout family</a>, under constant pressure from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>. In 1333 the bishop sold his rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-count-of-flanders">Louis of Nevers</a>, count of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a>, and after disputes with Brabant the town became a distinct lordship, one of the seventeen provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mechelen was a prosperous cloth town with a tall cathedral tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-rumbolds-cathedral">St Rumbold</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burgundian-state">Burgundian State</a> it became seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-mechelen">Parliament of Mechelen</a> in 1473, later the Great Council, the supreme court of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-york">Margaret of York</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-austria-duchess-of-savoy">Margaret of Austria</a>, regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, kept their courts there, making it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1559 Mechelen became the seat of the new archbishop, primate of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. It stayed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austrian-netherlands">Austrian Netherlands</a> until French annexation in 1795, and is today a city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-mechelen.jpg" alt="Lordship of Mechelen" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Lordship_of_Mechelen_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1333 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mechelen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis of Nevers, Margaret of Austria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mechelen, Malines</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1564&amp;focus=lordship-of-mechelen" aria-label="Lordship of Mechelen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4782 408 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Mechelen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5102 664L5073 659M5102 664L5118 774M5102 664L5102 664M5102 664L5099 670M5102 664L5099 670"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5073 659h0M5118 774h0M5102 664h0M5099 670h0M5099 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5102 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5102 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mechelen. <a href="/atlas?year=1564&amp;focus=lordship-of-mechelen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

